Q: Django sign up page shows alert &quot;Something went wrong, try again&quot; instead of success message A: <p>As I see, you had multiple small issues, not just one.</p>
<p><strong>In views.py:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p>Typo: <code>errror=&quot;yes&quot;</code> → should be <code>error=&quot;yes&quot;</code></p>
</li>
<li><p><code>d</code> was only defined in <code>except</code> but used in <code>return</code></p>
</li>
<li><p><code>return</code> indentation was wrong</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>type=student</code> → should be a string: <code>type=&quot;student&quot;</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>In the template:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>{% if user error == &quot;no&quot; %}</code> → <code>{% if error == &quot;no&quot; %}</code></p>
</li>
<li><p><code>window.location(...)</code> → <code>window.location.href = ...</code></p>
</li>
<li><p><code>{% if error ==&quot;yes&quot;}</code> → missing <code>%</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>So the views.py shall be:</p>
<pre><code>def user_signup(request):
    error = &quot;&quot;
    d = {'error': error}

    if request.method == 'POST':
        try:
            user = User.objects.create_user(
                first_name=request.POST['fname'],
                last_name=request.POST['lname'],
                username=request.POST['email'],
                password=request.POST['pwd']
            )
            StudentUser.objects.create(
                user=user,
                mobile=request.POST['contact'],
                image=request.FILES['images'],
                gender=request.POST['gender'],
                type=&quot;student&quot;
            )
            error = &quot;no&quot;
        except Exception:
            error = &quot;yes&quot;

        d = {'error': error}

    return render(request, 'user_signup.html', d)
</code></pre>
<p>and for template:</p>
<pre><code>{% if error == &quot;no&quot; %}
&lt;script&gt;
    alert(&quot;Inscription réussie !&quot;);
    window.location.href = &quot;{% url 'user_login' %}&quot;;
&lt;/script&gt;
{% endif %}

{% if error == &quot;yes&quot; %}
&lt;script&gt;alert(&quot;Erreur, réessayez&quot;);&lt;/script&gt;
{% endif %}
</code></pre>
<p>That's all as I think</p>

Q: Custom properties is not included in Serilog messages when other variables are included A: <p>You should use the message template to property pass the Application parameter.</p>
<p>From Serilog <a href="https://github.com/serilog/serilog/wiki/Writing-Log-Events#message-template-syntax" rel="nofollow noreferrer">docs</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If any of the property names are non-numeric, then all property names will be matched from left-to-right with the log method's parameters</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To avoid this mix with properties and named parameters, they use output templates which can use built-in properties, or custom ones and this way they apply to all messages. If you need more than this, you can use <a href="https://github.com/serilog/serilog/wiki/Configuration-Basics#enrichers" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Enrichers</a>, but they still have to work with the template. Basically your messages must only contain properties (named or indexed) that will match to a provided parameter.</p>
<p>In <code>appsettings.json</code> add:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>&quot;WriteTo&quot;: [
  {
    &quot;Name&quot;: &quot;Console&quot;,
    &quot;Args&quot;: {
      &quot;outputTemplate&quot;: &quot;{Timestamp:HH:mm} [{Level}] [{Application}] {Message}{NewLine}{Exception}&quot;
    }
  }
],
</code></pre>
<p>or if you want this by code:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Log.Logger = new LoggerConfiguration()
    .ReadFrom.Configuration(configuration)
    .WriteTo.Console(outputTemplate: &quot;{Timestamp:HH:mm} [{Level}] [{Application}] {Message}{NewLine}{Exception}&quot;)
    .CreateLogger();

</code></pre>
<p>This defines your template for your messages. <code>Level</code>,<code>Application</code> etc all get bound to known properties and it applies to all <code>Log*</code> function calls.</p>
<p>Your logging now becomes:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Log.Information(&quot;This is my message&quot;);

Log.Warning(&quot;This is my message with ekstra property {number}&quot;, &quot;111&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>And the output is:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>10:46 [Information] [MyAppName] This is my message
10:46 [Warning] [MyAppName] This is my message with ekstra property &quot;111&quot;
</code></pre>
